  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1228 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nBroad upper trough over the Great Basin will translate eastward \nacross CO Monday and Monday night with snow spreading into the \nContinental Divide Monday morning, and across the valleys into the \nsoutheast mountains by afternoon.  Isolated to scattered showers \nwill spill off the mountains into the adjacent plains Monday night \nwith the greatest coverage south of highway 50.  Overall, system \nlooks lacking for moisture, leading to only some light snow \naccumulations across the mountains, and a spotty trace to under an \ninch of snow across the plains.  Timing of the upper trough passage \nat night across the plains will lead to lower snow levels and a \nbetter chance for lower elevation communities to at least see some \nsnow flakes.  However lacking much in the way of upslope and \nmoisture, any snow amounts will be spotty and light with \naccumulations limited to cold surfaces.  Not anticipating much in \nthe way of travel impacts east of the I-25 corridor.  \n\nIt will dry out again for Tuesday and much of Wednesday with below \nnormal temperatures gradually warming towards mid week.  Models are \ncoming into better consensus with the next system for late week, \nwith energy splitting as it dives to the south into Old Mexico. The \nnorthern stream trough, which still varies in amplitude and timing \nbetween the long range models and ensembles, moves across CO around \nThanksgiving.  Trend is still towards a drier and warmer forecast \nfor much of the region late week into next weekend, though the \ncentral mountains could pick up some light snow accumulations with \nthe passing system.",
